WEBVTT

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:10.039
What happened was I knew I was writing it and I remember that Rob Greenberg was in the room, the writer's room, and we were in there and we were like, it'll be great, it'll be great.
发生的事情是我知道我正在写它，我记得罗布·格林伯格在房间里，作家的房间里，我们在那里，我们就像，这会很棒，会很棒。

00:00:10.039 --> 00:00:14.039
She'll write the wrong name and then a real call in that name all throughout the script.
她会写错名字，然后在整个剧本中都用这个名字进行真正的呼叫。

00:00:14.039 --> 00:00:25.070
And I either went to the bathroom or went to meet with you guys in your office for like one second and I came back in and Rob said, oh, we have a name that they could have written down.
我要么去了洗手间，要么去办公室和你们见面了大约一秒钟，然后我回来，罗布说，哦，我们有一个他们可以写下来的名字。

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:25.910
Okay, great.
好的，太好了。

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:28.070
Well, this is, he goes swarly.
嗯，这就是，他变得暴躁了。

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:30.690
And I said, you don't need to tell me anything else.
我说，你不需要告诉我任何其他事情。

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:33.350
That's, I've got this.
那就是，我有这个。

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:36.779
I'm alone, E.I.
我独自一人，E.I.

00:00:36.779 --> 00:00:39.979
won't be soon.
不会很快。

00:00:39.979 --> 00:00:49.939
In New York City, when I see you, please permit me to tell you everything.
在纽约，当我见到你时，请允许我告诉你一切。

00:00:49.939 --> 00:00:53.579
In New York City.
在纽约市。

00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:56.740
Well, hello, welcome to another episode of How We Made Your Brother.
好吧，你好，欢迎收看《我们如何造就你的兄弟》的另一集。

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:57.579
I'm Josh Radner.
我是乔什·拉德纳。

00:00:57.579 --> 00:00:58.780
I'm here with my friend Craig Thomas.
我和我的朋友克雷格·托马斯在这里。

00:00:58.780 --> 00:00:59.780
Hello, Craig.
你好，克雷格。

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:00.780
Hey, Josh.
嘿，乔什。

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.099
Craig co-created a show with Carter Bayes called How I Met Your Mother.
克雷格与卡特·贝叶斯共同创作了一部名为《老爸老妈的浪漫史》的节目。

00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:09.060
I started on that show as Ted Mosby from 2005 to 2014.
从 2005 年到 2014 年，我开始在这部剧中饰演 Ted Mosby。

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.180
We did 208 episodes of the show.
我们制作了 208 集的节目。

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.900
We're making our way through them one by one.
我们正在一一地解决它们。

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:19.819
We also talk a lot about what went into every decision.
我们还讨论了很多关于每个决定的因素。

00:01:19.819 --> 00:01:21.739
We're getting pretty granular.
我们变得非常精细。

00:01:21.739 --> 00:01:28.420
Craig and Carter, of course, were joined by a murderer's row of fantastic writers.
当然，克雷格和卡特身边还有一群出色的作家。

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:31.420
And we have one of the best here with us today.
今天我们这里有最优秀的人之一。

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.859
Craig say a little bit more to tee it up.
克雷格又说了一些话来准备一下。

00:01:33.859 --> 00:01:34.859
Oh, yes.
哦是的。

00:01:34.859 --> 00:01:49.060
This gentleman joined us in season two with a very intimidating resume joining me and Carter who had never even been on staff on a multi-camera sitcom, technically speaking, and Greg joined us and I have already blown the name.
这位绅士在第二季加入了我们，他的简历非常令人生畏，从技术上讲，我和卡特甚至从未在多机位情景喜剧中担任过工作人员，而格雷格也加入了我们，我已经名声大噪了。

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:52.099
Ladies and gentlemen, it's Greg Maylands, let's just get right into it.
女士们先生们，我是格雷格·梅兰兹，让我们开始吧。

00:01:52.099 --> 00:01:58.666
Greg Maylands, who worked on so many great shows, including Friends and co-running or running friends for several years.
格雷格·梅兰兹（Greg Maylands）曾制作过许多精彩节目，其中包括《老友记》以及多年来共同运行或运行的朋友。

00:01:58.666 --> 00:02:09.391
I mean, Greg was a name we would see when Alec, I went to college with Alec, we would watch friends in college and like see Greg's name on there who was very young writing for friends at the time.
我的意思是，当我和亚历克一起上大学时，格雷格是我们会看到的名字，我们会在大学里看朋友，喜欢看到格雷格的名字在那里，当时他还很年轻为朋友写作。

00:02:09.391 --> 00:02:17.379
And we'd, I would think to myself someday, maybe I'll just at least get to meet maybe a guy like Greg Maylands and just like talk to him one time.
我们，我会想，有一天，也许我至少会遇到像格雷格·梅兰兹这样的人，并与他交谈一次。

00:02:17.379 --> 00:02:27.219
Instead, Greg joined season two of how much mother and did multiple seasons helping us as incredibly valuable and wonderful and all, not just a great writer, but just a great dude.
相反，格雷格加入了《多么母亲》的第二季，并做了多个赛季的工作，帮助我们变得非常有价值和精彩，不仅是一个伟大的作家，而且还是一个伟大的家伙。

00:02:27.219 --> 00:02:29.528
Let's, let's keep complimenting him for 45 minutes.
让我们继续赞美他45分钟。

00:02:29.528 --> 00:02:30.659
I don't want him to talk.
我不想让他说话。

00:02:30.659 --> 00:02:33.780
I want to throw, I want to throw one more thing out there about that.
我想扔掉，我想再扔掉一件与此有关的事情。

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.294
This is Greg, by the way, acclamate to his voice.
顺便说一句，这是格雷格，对他的声音鼓掌。

00:02:37.294 --> 00:02:37.939
Go ahead.
前进。

00:02:37.939 --> 00:02:46.159
The preamble to working on the show was I was on Will and Grace the year before, when you guys did your first season of Will and Grace.
制作该剧的序言是前一年我在《威尔与格蕾丝》中，当时你们正在制作《威尔与格蕾丝》的第一季。

00:02:46.159 --> 00:03:10.025
And there were three writers on Will and Grace, and we were all, you know, it was a cliche, I used to have put, there were three of us who were watching how I met your mother from the first episode all the way through and we'd come in the next day and the writers would talk about the episodes and stuff and those and how much we loved the show and this was the last season of Will and Grace.
《威尔和格蕾丝》有三位编剧，我们都是，你知道，这是陈词滥调，我曾经说过，我们三个人从第一集开始一直在看我如何遇见你的母亲，我们第二天就来了，编剧们会谈论剧集和东西以及我们有多喜欢这个节目，这是《威尔和格蕾丝》的最后一季。

00:03:10.025 --> 00:03:23.419
So I was already like looking for what could be next for me but it was Jamie Ronheimer, Robbie Rashid and me all on Will and Grace talking about how I met your mother, all of us ended up writing on how I met your mother.
所以我已经在寻找我的下一步，但杰米·朗海默、罗比·拉希德和我都在《威尔和格蕾丝》中谈论我如何遇见你母亲，我们所有人最终都写下了我如何遇见你母亲。

00:03:23.419 --> 00:03:24.620
That's so cool.
太酷了。

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:27.099
For multiple seasons, I forgot that little trivia fact.
在很多季中，我都忘记了这个小事实。

00:03:27.099 --> 00:03:27.599
Yeah.
是的。

00:03:27.599 --> 00:03:28.675
That's amazing.
太棒了。

00:03:28.675 --> 00:03:40.793
And Greg, you kind of sought us out a little bit and made us look very good to our bosses because you were a way bigger name than we were and we were not a hit show yet.
格雷格，你有点寻找我们，让我们在老板面前看起来非常好，因为你的名字比我们大得多，而且我们还不是热门节目。

00:03:40.793 --> 00:03:42.084
It was a big show.
这是一场盛大的表演。

00:03:42.084 --> 00:03:42.801
It wasn't.
事实并非如此。

00:03:42.801 --> 00:03:43.589
It was not.
事实并非如此。

00:03:43.589 --> 00:03:46.601
It was season one was okay and it did not.
第一季还可以，但事实并非如此。

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:57.669
I love the show, the biggest thing for me was if I could write on this show I would learn so much.
我喜欢这个节目，对我来说最重要的是，如果我能在这个节目上写作，我会学到很多东西。

00:03:57.669 --> 00:04:00.005
It was a show like I've never seen.
这是一场我从未见过的表演。

00:04:00.005 --> 00:04:09.750
The way you guys created it essentially in television, how to tell a story in a very, very different way than it had ever been done on television.
你们在电视上创造故事的方式基本上是如何以一种与电视上以前非常非常不同的方式讲述故事的。

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:18.499
I mean, on Friends, we did seven flashbacks maybe, 10 years, but I was like, it seemed like I could learn so much from you guys.
我的意思是，在《老友记》中，我们可能做了七次闪回，十年了，但我当时想，似乎我可以从你们身上学到很多东西。

00:04:18.499 --> 00:04:24.857
And that's why I pursued it because I didn't have, you know, I was open and I loved the show.
这就是我追求它的原因，因为我没有，你知道，我是开放的，我喜欢这个节目。

00:04:24.857 --> 00:04:27.660
So it seemed like it made a lot of sense.
所以这似乎很有意义。

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:39.335
Greg, given that you, you had such a history before how I met my mother writing on more kind of classically structured sitcoms, did you, before you saw how I met your mother, Did you feel narratively kind of hemmed in?
格雷格，考虑到你，在我遇见我母亲之前，你有这样的历史，在更经典的结构情景喜剧中写作，在你看到我如何遇见你母亲之前，你是否感到叙事上受到了某种束缚？

00:04:39.335 --> 00:04:44.369
Like, or did how I make your mother show you that there was more possible to do with the form?
就像，或者我如何让你母亲向你展示了表格还有更多的可能性？

00:04:44.369 --> 00:04:46.779
The second one, definitely not the first one.
第二个，绝对不是第一个。

00:04:46.779 --> 00:04:47.279
Right.
正确的。

00:04:47.279 --> 00:04:54.714
Yeah, but look, we talked about this off air, but it goes back to my desire to not stagnate.
是的，但是你看，我们在广播中讨论过这个问题，但这又回到了我不停滞不前的愿望。

00:04:54.714 --> 00:05:07.564
And if I could learn and grow and discover a whole new way of writing, which I'll be totally honest, I'm not sure I ever got as good as you guys at doing that.
如果我能够学习、成长并发现一种全新的写作方式，我会完全诚实，我不确定我是否能像你们那样擅长。

00:05:07.564 --> 00:05:08.776
But I got good.
但我很好。

00:05:08.776 --> 00:05:10.635
Like I was much better.
就像我好多了。

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:11.792
Do you know what I love?
你知道我爱什么吗？

00:05:11.792 --> 00:05:14.527
One of the many things I love about you, I'll keep calm.
我爱你的众多原因之一就是我会保持冷静。

00:05:14.527 --> 00:05:17.067
I'm just going to compliment you for the whole hour.
我只是要赞美你整整一个小时。

00:05:17.067 --> 00:05:22.879
You could have come in, and by the way, the network and the studio probably would have loved you coming in saying this.
你本来可以进来的，顺便说一句，网络和工作室可能会喜欢你进来说这句话。

00:05:22.879 --> 00:05:26.396
You could have come in and said, let's make this even more like friends.
你本可以进来说，让我们让这更像朋友吧。

00:05:26.396 --> 00:05:27.519
Friends was a huge hit.
《老友记》大受欢迎。

00:05:27.519 --> 00:05:29.180
Let's make this more like friends.
让我们让这更像朋友吧。

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:43.206
You came in instead and didn't see all the similarities of how much a mother to friends, which there are many and we love from, like I said, Alec, our producer and I watched friends together in our college living room and our house we lived in every week.
相反，你进来后并没有看到母亲与朋友之间有多少相似之处，我们有很多朋友，就像我说的，亚历克、我们的制片人和我每周都会在我们大学的客厅和我们住的房子里看到朋友们在一起。

00:05:43.206 --> 00:05:49.586
We love friends with all respect, but like we didn't want how I met your mother to just, and try to mimic that show.
我们非常尊重朋友，但就像我们不希望我遇见你母亲的方式只是，并试图模仿那个节目。

00:05:49.586 --> 00:05:51.402
We wanted it to be its own thing.
我们希望它成为自己的东西。

00:05:51.402 --> 00:05:57.837
You came in and really saw what was different about how I met your mother and said, do more of that, do more of that.
你进来后，真的看到了我遇见你母亲的不同之处，并说，多做一些，多做一些。

00:05:57.837 --> 00:06:02.953
And it kind of, watching this episode, Swarley, that's the episode we're talking about today.
就好像，观看这一集，斯沃利，这就是我们今天讨论的这一集。

00:06:02.953 --> 00:06:04.108
They're very classic.
它们非常经典。

00:06:04.108 --> 00:06:07.353
Greg's first of several episodes he wrote and an all-timer.
格雷格编写的几集中的第一集，也是他的历史之作。

00:06:07.353 --> 00:06:13.789
And I love how this episode begins with a cold open that very much differentiates friends from how I met your mother.
我喜欢这一集以冷漠的开场方式开始，这与我认识你母亲的方式截然不同。

00:06:13.789 --> 00:06:17.694
And it was sort of, I felt that that encapsulated your entire approach.
我觉得这概括了你的整个方法。

00:06:17.694 --> 00:06:21.159
You said, no, be your own thing, and I want to be part of that.
你说，不，做你自己的事，我想成为其中的一部分。

00:06:21.159 --> 00:06:21.819
I love that.
我喜欢那个。

00:06:21.819 --> 00:06:24.459
Thank you for that coming in with that attitude.
感谢您以这种态度前来。

00:06:24.459 --> 00:06:40.147
I got to talk about that for a second, because I did not write it in that way at all, like I just wrote it, but it seemed like they usually drink beer and in this scene they had to, because of the wrong name on the coffee cup, they had to be in a coffee shop.
我得谈谈这一点，因为我根本没有那样写，就像我刚刚写的那样，但看起来他们通常喝啤酒，在这个场景中他们不得不喝，因为咖啡杯上的名字错误，他们必须在一家咖啡店。

00:06:40.147 --> 00:06:42.752
What's a good joke that can come from that?
这能开出什么好笑话呢？

00:06:42.752 --> 00:06:47.477
And I just thought saying, well, drinking beer is better than drinking coffee.
我只是想说，好吧，喝啤酒比喝咖啡更好。

00:06:47.477 --> 00:06:49.415
It wasn't even thinking of that.
它甚至没有想到这一点。

00:06:49.415 --> 00:06:55.351
I forgot about that because we had a lot of the same crew as friends did on how I met your mother.
我忘记了这一点，因为我们有很多和朋友们在我如何认识你母亲时相同的工作人员。

00:06:55.351 --> 00:07:04.134
And I got down onto the stage that first day and a couple of the cameramen who I knew from friends were like, oh, that was a good dig at friends.
第一天我走上舞台，我从朋友那里认识的几个摄影师就说，哦，这对朋友来说是一个很好的挖苦。

00:07:04.134 --> 00:07:06.193
This is a good dig at the friends.
这对朋友来说是一个很好的挖苦。

00:07:06.193 --> 00:07:08.677
And I was like, wait, what is a good dig?
我当时想，等等，什么是好的挖掘？

00:07:08.677 --> 00:07:12.190
And they pointed it out and I was like, oh, yeah, totally.
他们指出了这一点，我当时想，哦，是的，完全如此。

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:14.310
I can see why you would think that.
我明白你为什么会这么想。

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.066
But it didn't come from that.
但它并非由此而来。

00:07:16.066 --> 00:07:20.003
I think it's pretty universally acknowledged as a dig at friends.
我认为这是普遍认为的对朋友的挖苦。

00:07:20.003 --> 00:07:44.716
Like, I don't think anyone thinks of it that it's not that all I can tell you is I wasn't there might have been some you were subconsciously like Freudian like killing your father or something like you were yeah I know I love in this episode though that it starts with a friends joke and ends with a cheers joke yeah and there's something so great about that that you know in some ways we were a humble show.
就像，我不认为有人会想到这一点，我能告诉你的是，我不存在，可能有一些你下意识地像弗洛伊德一样杀了你的父亲，或者像你一样的东西是的，我知道我喜欢这一集，虽然它以一个朋友的笑话开始，以一个欢呼的笑话结束，是的，有一些很棒的东西，你知道在某些方面我们是一个谦虚的节目。

00:07:44.716 --> 00:07:46.776
We were trying to stay on the air.
我们试图继续播出。

00:07:46.776 --> 00:07:53.802
We were, but also, I think we were really trying to situate ourselves in the constellation of great classic sitcoms.
我们确实是，而且，我认为我们真的试图将自己置于伟大的经典情景喜剧中。

00:07:53.802 --> 00:07:54.302
Yeah.
是的。

00:07:54.302 --> 00:07:54.802
Yeah.
是的。

00:07:54.802 --> 00:08:02.737
And even the, we can get there later, but the ending with the Cheers font is so funny and such a little wink towards sitcom lovers.
甚至，我们可以稍后到达那里，但是以干杯字体结尾的结尾是如此有趣，并且对情景喜剧爱好者来说有点眨眼。

00:08:02.737 --> 00:08:03.948
It made me so happy.
这让我很高兴。

00:08:03.948 --> 00:08:07.825
And your name's in there because Greg was an executive producer.
你的名字就在那里，因为格雷格是执行制片人。

00:08:07.825 --> 00:08:10.430
Greg's name is up there in the Cheers logo.
格雷格的名字出现在干杯标志中。

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:14.064
That made me happier than in anything in the entire episode.
这让我比整集中的任何事情都更高兴。

00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:16.670
We'll seeing that before we go on.
在我们继续之前我们会看到这一点。

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:22.790
Let's just say this episode is called Swarly and Alec, when did this episode air?
我们就说这一集叫做斯沃利和亚历克，这一集什么时候播出？

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:25.350
December 8th, 2006.
2006 年 12 月 8 日。

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:27.389
December 8th, 2006.
2006 年 12 月 8 日。

00:08:27.389 --> 00:08:33.190
And Craig, or maybe even Greg, you can give like a little summation of what is this episode.
克雷格，或者甚至格雷格，你可以对这一集做一些总结。

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:34.029
What happens?
会发生什么？

00:08:34.029 --> 00:08:37.470
Clicky log line for people who didn't just watch it the way that we'd just watch it.
对于那些不只是像我们那样观看它的人来说，点击日志行。

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:40.803
It was a really big episode impactful important.
这是一个非常重要的重要事件。

00:08:40.803 --> 00:08:50.665
And I remember fighting to be able to write it because I really wanted to write it, because Marshall and Lily have been broken up for so long.
我记得我为了写它而奋斗，因为我真的很想写它，因为马歇尔和莉莉已经分手这么久了。

00:08:50.665 --> 00:08:55.387
And this was the episode where they were going to get back together.
这就是他们要复合的那一集。

00:08:55.387 --> 00:08:58.582
And that one I really, really wanted to write.
我真的非常想写那一篇。

00:08:58.582 --> 00:09:02.610
And I remember fighting for it and was granted the script.
我记得我为之奋斗并获得了剧本。

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:05.180
And so I was really excited about it.
所以我对此感到非常兴奋。

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:11.569
