Do you know Brian Boyle?
你认识布莱恩·博伊尔吗？

00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:02.080
Yeah, Brian.
是的，布莱恩。

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:05.328
I wrote on American dad with Carter and I with Brian Boyle.
我和卡特一起写《美国爸爸》，我和布莱恩·博伊尔一起写。

00:18:05.328 --> 00:18:07.200
Yeah, you guys go way back, right?
是啊，你们都回去了，对吧？

00:18:07.279 --> 00:18:18.920
Brian did that to well Brian and I went to middle school together, but He put on everything Sebastian Jones owned who's also a writer He was on Spin City and other things.
布莱恩这样做是为了布莱恩和我一起上中学，但他穿上了塞巴斯蒂安·琼斯拥有的一切，他也是一名作家，他在旋转城市和其他东西上。

00:18:18.920 --> 00:18:19.759
Sebastian.
塞巴斯蒂安.

00:18:19.759 --> 00:18:22.279
You all became comedy writers from that event.
你们都从那次事件中成为了喜剧作家。

00:18:22.279 --> 00:18:23.119
Oh my God.
我的天啊。

00:18:23.119 --> 00:18:28.359
There was a time when friends were a quarter of the writing staff all went to high school together.
曾经有一段时间，朋友占写作人员的四分之一，他们都一起上高中。

00:18:28.359 --> 00:18:29.200
Wow.
哇。

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:30.039
Oh my God.
我的天啊。

00:18:30.039 --> 00:18:31.160
That's crazy.
太疯狂了。

00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:42.960
Greg, I remember there were two things you told me early when you came in, maybe the first couple of years, but one of them was you said it's really important for writers to get to know the actors.
格雷格，我记得你刚进来时（也许是头几年）就告诉过我两件事，但其中之一是你说对于编剧来说了解演员非常重要。

00:18:42.960 --> 00:19:07.240
Like it's really, like writers and actors should be, you know, you know, really, because you take, you get to know them, you get little, you know, you notice little quirks about their, the way they say things or the way they throw lines away that if you study them, and that was, that was part of what in the negotiation around the writers was like, writers should be on set, writers should be fraternizing with the actors.
真的，就像作家和演员应该那样，你知道，你知道，真的，因为你了解他们，你得到的很少，你知道，你注意到他们的小怪癖，他们说话的方式或他们扔台词的方式，如果你研究他们，那就是，这就是围绕编剧谈判的一部分，编剧应该在片场，编剧应该与演员友好相处。

00:19:07.240 --> 00:19:09.920
It's like magic happens because of that, right?
就像魔法因此发生一样，对吧？

00:19:09.920 --> 00:19:10.920
That was one thing you told me.
这是你告诉我的一件事。

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:22.519
The other thing was you know when all guns are firing on a sitcom, when you can picture any two characters, put them in a room together, and you know you can kind of see the scene.
另一件事是，当情景喜剧中所有枪声都开火时，你可以想象任何两个角色，将他们放在一个房间里，你知道你可以看到场景。

00:19:22.519 --> 00:19:23.920
Do you remember saying that?
你还记得这么说过吗？

00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:26.000
Yeah, I don't think that was...
是的，我不认为那是...

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:26.839
Stephen Lloyd.
史蒂芬·劳埃德.

00:19:26.839 --> 00:19:27.680
Maybe Stephen Lloyd.
也许是史蒂芬·劳埃德。

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:37.160
Yeah, I think yeah, that maybe kind of paraphrase something that Chuck Laurie once famously said, which is a sitcom is two people on a couch talking.
是的，我想是的，这也许是对查克·劳瑞曾经说过的一句名言的解释，情景喜剧是两个人坐在沙发上聊天。

00:19:37.160 --> 00:19:39.039
Right, right.
对，对。

00:19:39.039 --> 00:19:41.039
But yeah, I believe that.
但是，是的，我相信这一点。

00:19:41.039 --> 00:19:49.279
But once you get the and the characters on how many whether we're so great, you know, and we got to have so much fun with Robin after season one.
但是一旦你知道我们有多少个角色，我们是否那么伟大，你知道，第一季结束后我们必须和罗宾一起玩得很开心。

00:19:49.279 --> 00:19:49.779
Yeah.
是的。

00:19:49.779 --> 00:19:50.279
Yeah.
是的。

00:19:50.200 --> 00:19:53.000
Yeah, we found so many other speeds for her in season two.
是的，我们在第二季中为她找到了很多其他的速度。

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:56.160
And I was the first thing I ever said to them on the phone.
我是我在电话里对他们说的第一句话。

00:19:56.160 --> 00:19:57.200
Do you remember that?
你还记得吗？

00:19:57.200 --> 00:19:58.039
What did you say?
你说什么？

00:19:58.039 --> 00:20:01.920
I said, Robin should have had been a one-hit wonder in Canada.
我说，罗宾在加拿大应该是一击成名的奇迹。

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:03.160
Yes, that's right.
是的，没错。

00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:05.039
The initial Robin Sparkle scene.
罗宾·斯帕克最初的场景。

00:20:05.039 --> 00:20:06.039
Yeah, it was you.
是的，就是你。

00:20:06.039 --> 00:20:06.960
It was like, it was you.
就像，那就是你。

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:09.279
I said Robin Sprinkles.
我说罗宾·斯普林尔斯。

00:20:09.279 --> 00:20:10.440
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
是啊，是啊，是啊。

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:14.240
And then you said Robin Sparkles, and I was like, oh, yeah, that's better.
然后你说罗宾·斯帕克斯，我当时想，哦，是的，那更好了。

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:16.039
I mean, yeah, that's true.
我的意思是，是的，确实如此。

00:20:16.039 --> 00:20:20.319
I mean, that was, we're getting a little ahead of ourselves here, but it's true a little bit, not too far down the line.
我的意思是，我们在这里有点超前了，但确实有一点，不是太远。

00:20:20.319 --> 00:20:23.079
Because Robin didn't have any backstory and see some more.
因为罗宾没有任何背景故事，也没有看到更多。

00:20:23.079 --> 00:20:25.359
Yeah, and you said, how do you think you arrived at that?
是的，你说，你认为你是如何做到这一点的？

00:20:25.359 --> 00:20:29.200
Can you just, on Canada backstory, the Alanis Morissette thing?
你能简单讲一下加拿大的背景故事，艾拉妮丝·莫莉塞特（Alanis Morissette）的事情吗？

00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:30.559
Like, we'll just dive, we'll just do it.
就像，我们只是潜水，我们就这么做。

00:20:30.559 --> 00:20:33.119
Yeah, yeah, something like that, yeah.
是的，是的，类似的事情，是的。

00:20:33.119 --> 00:20:34.359
It's such a great idea.
这是个好主意。

00:20:34.359 --> 00:20:36.000
I mean, it's one of the all-time best ideas.
我的意思是，这是有史以来最好的想法之一。

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:43.000
There's also like certain Canadian things, like what's the huge Canadian band, the Tragically Hip?
还有一些加拿大的东西，比如加拿大的巨大乐队“Tragically Hip”是什么？

00:20:43.319 --> 00:20:44.160
Do you know the Tragically Hip?
你知道悲剧性的髋关节吗？

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:45.000
Yeah.
是的。

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:51.359
They're the biggest band Canada ever saw, and they would play very small clubs the rest of the world, including the United States.
他们是加拿大有史以来最大的乐队，他们会在世界其他地方的很小的俱乐部演出，包括美国。

00:20:51.359 --> 00:20:55.200
Like they never broke out of Canada really, even though we're so beloved.
就像他们从未真正突破加拿大一样，尽管我们如此相爱。

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:58.319
I think there's something funny about like art just being like sequestered up.
我认为艺术被孤立起来是很有趣的。

00:20:58.319 --> 00:21:02.160
Like you live up here, it might not travel south, you know?
就像你住在这里一样，它可能不会向南移动，你知道吗？

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:06.680
You can really not have heard of things that happen up there, which made this, which is perfectly fed this.
你真的可能没有听说过那里发生的事情，它们造就了这个，它完美地喂养了这个。

00:21:06.680 --> 00:21:09.640
Some of our, some of our candidate jokes were my favorite.
我们的一些候选人笑话是我最喜欢的。

00:21:09.640 --> 00:21:11.319
I mean, oh my God.
我的意思是，天哪。

00:21:11.319 --> 00:21:13.519
Yeah, you, you helped to open the door.
是的，你，你帮忙开门。

00:21:13.519 --> 00:21:15.966
Well, she was Canadian before I came on the show, it wasn't me.
好吧，在我上节目之前她是加拿大人，不是我。

00:21:15.966 --> 00:21:16.466
Right.
正确的。

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:17.759
But just the one, one to think.
但只有一个，一个值得思考的。

00:21:17.759 --> 00:21:25.559
But the, one of the things, which is the kind of joke I love, which is she was talking, getting all excited about something.
但是，其中一件事是我喜欢的那种笑话，她在说话，对某件事感到兴奋。

00:21:25.559 --> 00:21:28.480
And after you went to hockey game, you know, you all go out to Dandies.
当你看完曲棍球比赛后，你知道，你们都会去花花公子那里。

00:21:29.480 --> 00:21:30.880
There's just silence.
只有沉默。

00:21:30.880 --> 00:21:32.240
She's like, oh, come on.
她就像，哦，来吧。

00:21:32.240 --> 00:21:34.000
You guys don't have Dandies here?
你们这里没有丹迪吗？

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:40.000
That thing of like people from another country, and that wasn't my joke in any way, but sure was Stephen Lloyd.
就像来自另一个国家的人一样，这绝不是我的笑话，但斯蒂芬·劳埃德确实是这样。

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:44.619
But that kind of joke just really makes me laugh.
但这种笑话真的让我发笑。

00:21:44.619 --> 00:21:50.099
Kobe always played the exasperation at not having the same cultural references, like very well.
科比总是表现出对没有相同文化参考的愤怒，就像很好的那样。

00:21:50.099 --> 00:21:50.940
Like, just.
就像，只是。

00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:52.380
Yeah, cause that was real.
是的，因为那是真实的。

00:21:52.380 --> 00:21:54.140
And she was like, wow, they do more Canada.
她说，哇，他们在加拿大做得更多。

00:21:54.140 --> 00:21:57.299
That was an actor playing the reality.
那是一个演员在扮演现实。

00:21:57.299 --> 00:22:34.460
Greg, I thought you were gonna say one of the earliest things you said to us what that made me that made me it was when we met with you about you maybe working on the show and I go this is the guy you said I love that you have a couple that loves each other on this show you have a couple that's not the bicker sins it's not like the typical TV couple and it's the sort of heavy said guy and the wife who's taking the piss out of him is like they actually really like each other and it's funny because you join then you join the show and they were apart and And I think that's why you wanted to write the episode that got them back together, isn't it?
格雷格，我以为你会说你对我们说的最早的话之一是什么让我成为了我，当我们见到你的时候，关于你可能在节目中工作，我去，这就是你说的我爱的那个人，你们在这个节目中有一对彼此相爱的情侣，你们有一对不是争吵的罪孽，不像典型的电视情侣，是那种沉重的男人和让他生气的妻子，就像他们真的很喜欢对方一样这很有趣，因为你加入然后你加入节目，他们分开了，我想这就是为什么你想写一集让他们重新聚在一起，不是吗？

00:22:34.460 --> 00:22:55.339
Because that was one of the things we talked about at Will and Grits, and one of the things that I thought was the most challenging and something that I wanted to learn, because I took it a step further than what you're saying, that yes, there was a couple of them there that loved each other, and clearly were meant to be each other, yet they were funny, they were really funny in love.
因为这是我们在威尔和格里茨讨论的事情之一，也是我认为最具挑战性和我想学习的事情之一，因为我比你所说的更进一步，是的，那里有一对彼此相爱的人，显然注定要成为彼此，但他们很有趣，他们在爱情中真的很有趣。

00:22:55.339 --> 00:23:03.980
And if you remember on Friends, we got Ross and Rachel together and then broke them up as fast as we possibly could because they weren't nearly as funny together as they were apart.
如果你还记得在《老友记》中，我们让罗斯和瑞秋在一起，然后尽可能快地把他们分开，因为他们在一起并不像分开时那么有趣。

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:04.819
Right.
正确的。

00:23:04.819 --> 00:23:09.450
So to see a couple that is funny together was a revelation to me.
所以看到一对有趣的情侣在一起对我来说是一个启示。

00:23:09.450 --> 00:23:12.329
And I was like, I gotta learn how to do that.
我想，我必须学习如何做到这一点。

00:23:12.329 --> 00:23:13.569
Was it a bait and switch though?
这是一个诱饵和开关吗？

00:23:13.569 --> 00:23:16.450
Did you sign up to wanna be on the show and then we broke them up?
你是不是报名参加这个节目然后我们就分手了？

00:23:16.450 --> 00:23:18.690
Did Eric had me broken them up already?
埃里克已经让我把他们拆散了吗？

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:20.490
No, they broke up at the end of season one.
不，他们在第一季结束时分手了。

00:23:20.490 --> 00:23:21.329
So they went.
于是他们就去了。

00:23:21.329 --> 00:23:23.210
I know, but we met Greg during season one.
我知道，但我们在第一季遇到了格雷格。

00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:24.130
I think didn't we, Greg?
我想我们不是吗，格雷格？

00:23:24.130 --> 00:23:26.049
Didn't we meet you as it was airing anyway?
无论如何，我们在播出时没有见过你吗？

00:23:26.049 --> 00:23:27.450
I might be misremembering.
我可能记错了。

00:23:27.450 --> 00:23:30.089
But you wanted to get them back together, which I love.
但你想让他们重新在一起，我喜欢这一点。

00:23:30.089 --> 00:23:32.890
You're talking about the fancy lunch at the commissary, right?
你说的是小卖部的丰盛午餐，对吗？

00:23:32.890 --> 00:23:33.890
We had a fancy commissary.
我们有一个很棒的小卖部。

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:34.890
I remember that.
我记得。

00:23:34.890 --> 00:23:35.890
Yeah.
是的。

00:23:35.890 --> 00:23:36.890
You see.
你看。

00:23:36.890 --> 00:23:37.890
I mean, we were nervous.
我的意思是，我们很紧张。

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:38.890
We were intimidated.
我们被吓到了。

00:23:38.890 --> 00:23:39.890
We were, I want to make clear.
我想澄清的是，我们是。

00:23:39.890 --> 00:23:42.089
Carter and I are both huge friends fans.
卡特和我都是朋友的忠实粉丝。

00:23:42.089 --> 00:23:44.690
Like, whatever that cold open was or wasn't to side.
就像，无论冷开是否在一边。

00:23:44.690 --> 00:23:47.690
We love friends and we were very intimidated by you.
我们爱朋友，但我们却被你吓到了。

00:23:47.690 --> 00:23:53.809
And we, you know, there was still the feeling from the studio on the network that these two guys, Carter and Craig, are very young.
我们，你知道，网络上的工作室仍然给人一种感觉，卡特和克雷格这两个家伙非常年轻。

00:23:53.809 --> 00:23:55.089
They've never run a TV show.
他们从未经营过电视节目。

00:23:55.089 --> 00:23:58.410
They never had, they never staffed on this real sitcom.
他们从来没有、也从未在这部真正的情景喜剧中配备过工作人员。

00:23:58.410 --> 00:24:08.009
So the confidence it gave them for you to join was very real, but then you came in and you were great and you wanted to keep making, help us make this show, not make it more like another show.
所以它给了他们让你加入的信心是非常真实的，但是当你进来的时候，你很棒，你想继续制作，帮助我们制作这个节目，而不是让它更像另一个节目。

00:24:08.009 --> 00:24:09.450
And again, that was huge.
再说一次，那是巨大的。

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:12.210
So proudest of any show I've ever worked on.
我对我参与过的所有节目感到最自豪。

00:24:12.210 --> 00:24:13.210
Oh, man.
哦，伙计。

00:24:13.210 --> 00:24:14.210
That's so nice.
那太好了。

00:24:14.210 --> 00:24:15.210
Great.
伟大的。

00:24:15.210 --> 00:24:16.210
How many seasons were you on the show?
你参加了几季的节目？

00:24:16.210 --> 00:24:18.009
Two, three, four, five?
二、三、四、五？

00:24:18.009 --> 00:24:19.009
Five.
五。

00:24:19.009 --> 00:24:20.009
Five films, right.
五部电影，对吧。

00:24:20.009 --> 00:24:22.529
And had you worked with Pam Fryman before on any of your shows?
您之前曾在自己的节目中与帕姆·弗莱曼合作过吗？

00:24:22.529 --> 00:24:23.529
Yeah.
是的。

00:24:23.529 --> 00:24:24.529
Yeah.
是的。

00:24:24.529 --> 00:24:25.529
She directed some friends.
她指导了一些朋友。

00:24:25.529 --> 00:24:26.529
Yeah.
是的。

00:24:26.529 --> 00:24:27.529
Okay.
好的。

00:24:27.529 --> 00:24:30.990
other friends writer as far as I can remember ever worked for him and from others.
据我所知，其他朋友作家曾为他和其他人工作过。

00:24:30.990 --> 00:24:31.990
So you are.
所以你是。

00:24:31.990 --> 00:24:32.990
Well, I wouldn't let that happen.
嗯，我不会让这种事发生。

00:24:32.990 --> 00:24:33.990
Obviously.
明显地。

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:35.990
Who asked we need a grant?
谁问我们需要补助金？

00:24:35.990 --> 00:24:36.990
That's cool.
这很酷。

00:24:36.990 --> 00:24:37.990
We should talk more about the episode too.
我们也应该更多地谈论这一集。

00:24:37.990 --> 00:24:38.990
Yeah.
是的。

00:24:38.990 --> 00:24:39.990
Let's talk about the episode.
我们来谈谈情节吧。

00:24:39.990 --> 00:24:40.990
So yes.
所以是的。

00:24:40.990 --> 00:24:46.509
So I love that it opens with this weather intentional, this friends thing.
所以我喜欢它以这样的天气、这样的朋友的方式开始。

00:24:46.509 --> 00:24:48.789
It ends with this cheers thing.
就以这件欢呼事结束了。

00:24:48.789 --> 00:24:56.029
To me, it was like just evidence like we were swinging for the fences, like we were really trying to make like a classic sitcom.
对我来说，这就像我们正在摇摆不定的证据，就像我们真的想拍一部经典情景喜剧一样。

00:24:56.029 --> 00:25:10.210
One thing that really amuses me about the whole Swarly bit, I think that it was really important to see Barney have episodes where he was losing and the butt of the joke.
关于斯沃利的整个过程，让我真正感到有趣的一件事是，我认为看到巴尼有输球和笑话的情节真的很重要。

00:25:10.210 --> 00:25:15.569
He couldn't be a triumphant character all the time.
他不可能一直都是一个胜利者。

